WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 27 Student Video
Unit 7, Lesson 3: Suffering

1
00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:12.770
In the midst of our suffering it's easy to feel alone, to feel isolated.

2
00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:13.690
When

3
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.400
we see or experience suffering it's natural to ask a lot of questions.

4
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:26.160
Why am I suffering? Am I alone? Is my suffering going to end?

5
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.980
Why is God allowing me to suffer?

6
00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:35.020
Where is God when I suffer and what is Christianity's unique response to

7
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.620
suffering and pain.

8
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.040
Let me share a story that might help.

9
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:46.220
In 1873, a priest by the name of Father Damien heard about a colony

10
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:50.980
of lepers who were living and dying without anybody to care for them.

11
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.800
So with full awareness of the risk, Father Damien went and became a priest

12
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:02.660
for this colony of lepers he prayed for them he preached to them he changed

13
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:08.160
their wounds he built caskets he buried many of them for 12 years with deep

14
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.660
compassion and care he loved this colony of lepers so they would know and

15
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:19.460
experience the love of God one Sunday evening however everything

16
00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:25.340
changed father Damien stood before the people with leprosy and he spoke these

17
00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:31.160
two words he said, we lepers. He opened up his robe and showed

18
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.120
the first physical signs of leprosy.

19
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.780
Father Damien had become a leper.

20
00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:42.320
Let me ask you a couple questions about the story.

21
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:48.540
First, how did the view of the lepers change towards Father Damien when he

22
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:53.440
became a leper? No doubt beforehand they knew that he loved them.

23
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:58.920
They saw his willingness to sacrifice everything for them, but now when he got

24
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.520
leprosy everything was different.

25
00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.860
Now he could understand them in a new way.

26
00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.380
They saw the depth of his sacrifice for them.

27
00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:10.600
He could empathize with them in a personal way.

28
00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:17.400
The second question is, is God's response to our suffering more like Father Damien

29
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:23.280
before he got leprosy or after? And if we stop and we think about the

30
00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.980
life, ministry, and death of Jesus, I think the answer is clear.

31
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:34.080
It's after. See, God didn't just send a prophet or an angel or a book as important

32
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.960
as those are. In the person of Jesus Christ, God came down personally

33
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:45.740
and has experienced our pain. We are not abandoned to our suffering and our

34
00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:51.060
pain. So part of the answer to where is God when I suffer, is that God is right

35
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.220
there suffering with us.

36
00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:57.640
Think about the symbols for the various world religions.

37
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:04.120
Got the statue of Buddha for Buddhism, the gate for Shintoism, the

38
00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:09.140
crescent and the sword for Islam, or the star of David for Judaism.

39
00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:15.700
Yet for Christianity, the most defining image, the most defining symbol is the

40
00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:20.980
cross. A picture of the creator of the universe stepped down in human flesh,

41
00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:26.980
suffering for us. We follow and worship a God who has suffered.

42
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:33.780
Isaiah the prophet said the coming Messiah would be a man of sorrows, familiar with

43
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:34.440
suffering.

44
00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:40.540
And the book of Hebrews, the author of Hebrews writes in chapter 4 that we do not

45
00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:45.780
follow a high priest who's unable to sympathize with us, But we have a high priest

46
00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.540
who's been tempted and tested in every way like we are.

47
00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.240
He understands our weaknesses, but is without sin.

48
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.840
If you're ever tempted to think that God doesn't understand your suffering or your

49
00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.560
pain, you might think again. For example, if you're in physical pain and you think,

50
00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:05.880
gosh, I'm in so much pain, God doesn't understand what I'm going through.

51
00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:11.540
Remember, Jesus was crucified and experienced excruciating physical

52
00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:15.940
pain. if you've gone through relational difficulties and maybe somebody's betrayed

53
00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:21.720
you or left you out and you think god you don't understand remember during his

54
00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:27.560
lifetime much of jesus family didn't believe in him and in fact he was betrayed by

55
00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.780
one of his closest disciples jesus understands

56
00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:38.980
many times what our suffering enables us to do is to have empathy for other people

57
00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:42.260
and build a deeper sense of community.

58
00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:48.220
Take the example of Father Damien contracting leprosy, as painful and as

59
00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:54.160
hurtful as that is. That enabled him to be into a new community and experience the

60
00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:57.360
love of a people that wasn't there before.

61
00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:04.160
Ultimately I might not be able to explain why you or me or anyone else

62
00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.300
experiences a particular kind of suffering.

63
00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:13.240
Sometimes we might know in this life, but many times it won't be until we get to

64
00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:16.480
eternity. But I know this. I know that God is good.

65
00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:20.040
I know that God has not abandoned us in our suffering.

66
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:25.800
I know God is asking you and me the same question he asked Job thousands of years

67
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.060
ago when Job was suffering immensely.

68
00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.760
He said, Job, will you trust me?

69
00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:36.400
It's important to remember that God can take our greatest pain, our greatest

70
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:42.340
suffering, and turn it to good. We see this in the life of Joseph, a story that you

71
00:05:42.340 --> 00:05:48.200
know. Joseph is sold into slavery from his own brothers, ends up in prison,

72
00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:54.020
but rises up through God's hand to be second in command in Egypt and is able to save

73
00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:59.640
his family from famine. When he finally talks with his brothers and they realize his

74
00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:03.660
identity, they're afraid that he might have retribution against them.

75
00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:05.960
But what does he say? He essentially says, don't worry.

76
00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:10.680
What you meant for evil, God meant for good.

77
00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:17.500
When Jesus was arrested and crucified, his apostles thought that all

78
00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:23.440
had been lost. This movement and person they had been following for roughly

79
00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.100
three years seemingly had come to an end.

80
00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:31.580
They were devastated. But what they didn't realize at that moment is in their

81
00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:35.940
darkest hour, God had never let go of the steering wheel.

82
00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.180
He was still in control and working his greatest good.

83
00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:45.480
We see it's through the resurrection that we know that death does not have the end.

84
00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:51.320
Suffering is not the end. And that this world will ultimately be

85
00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:51.880
redeemed.

86
00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:57.020
Suffering and pain do not have the final word.

87
00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.700
Jesus is risen. That's right, Jesus is risen.

88
00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:08.700
That's why the Apostle Paul, who experienced tremendous suffering, could shout with

89
00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:14.000
an air of defiance in 1 Corinthians 15, a chapter on the resurrection.

90
00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:19.000
Death, where's your victory? Death, where's your sting?

